{%- comment -%}
  Bassface collection page — replaces Dawn's main-collection-banner AND
  main-collection-product-grid for templates/collection.json.

  Two stock sections become one. Dawn splits the banner from the grid so a
  merchant can reorder or drop the banner, but the board draws a single column —
  breadcrumb, title, description, toolbar, grid — with the toolbar's count bound
  to the grid beneath it. Split across two sections that count would have to be
  recomputed in a section that cannot see the paginate block, and reordering
  them would put "12 Products" above nothing.

  Structurally this is bf-main-search with the search box removed and the
  collection's description in its place; the grid, Load More and empty state are
  the same treatment, which is what the boards draw. Where the two pages differ
  is that this one filters — see bf-filter-drawer.

  Load More is the Section Rendering API: bf-main-collection.js fetches the next
  page of this same section and appends its cards. `paginate` is what makes that
  honest — it knows both the true total and whether another page exists.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'bf-product-card.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-main-collection.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'bf-product-card.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-main-collection.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{%- comment -%}
  Loaded only where there is something to filter. A collection with no filters
  configured in Search & Discovery gets neither the trigger, the panel, nor the
  two files behind them.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if collection.filters.size > 0 -%}
  {{ 'bf-filter-drawer.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'bf-filter-drawer.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- liquid
  assign per_page = section.settings.products_per_page
-%}

{%- paginate collection.products by per_page -%}
  <bf-main-collection class="main-collection" data-section-id="{{ section.id }}">
    {%- comment -%}
      Both strings come from settings, not the locale file — same call as the
      search page. Dawn 15.5 has no `general.breadcrumbs` group, and adding one
      would mean editing en.default.json plus the thirty translations beside it
      to name two words the merchant can set here in seconds.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <nav class="main-collection__breadcrumb" aria-label="{{ section.settings.breadcrumb_label }}">
      <ol class="main-collection__crumbs">
        <li class="main-collection__crumb">
          <a class="main-collection__crumb-link" href="{{ routes.root_url }}">
            {{- section.settings.breadcrumb_home -}}
          </a>
        </li>
        <li class="main-collection__crumb" aria-hidden="true">/</li>
        <li class="main-collection__crumb" aria-current="page">{{ collection.title }}</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="main-collection__header">
      <h1 class="main-collection__title">{{ collection.title }}</h1>

      {%- comment -%}
        Rendered only when the merchant has written one. The board draws two
        lines here, but an empty <div> on a collection without a description
        would still take the 24px gap above it and leave a hole under the title.

        `collection.description` is rich text, so it arrives wrapped in <p> and
        may carry links — the CSS zeroes those margins rather than the Liquid
        stripping the tags, which would also strip the links.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if collection.description != blank -%}
        <div class="main-collection__description rte">{{ collection.description }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <div class="main-collection__content">
      {%- comment -%}
        The toolbar sits OUTSIDE the results region below, and that placement is
        the empty state's requirement rather than an accident of nesting:
        DESIGN-STATUS records that an empty collection keeps its toolbar so
        filters and sort stay reachable, reading "0 Products". Inside
        [data-results] it would be swapped away by the very filter change that
        emptied the grid, stranding the visitor with no way to undo it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="main-collection__toolbar">
        {%- comment -%}
          aria-live because filtering swaps the grid with no navigation and no
          focus move — without it the only feedback that anything happened is
          visual, and a screen-reader user ticking "In Stock" hears nothing at
          all. Polite, so it waits for a pause rather than interrupting.

          The raw figure rides along in a data attribute because the drawer's
          "Show Results (12)" needs the number, and the text beside it is
          "12 products" in English and something else in every other locale.
          Scraping digits out of a translated string is a bug in waiting.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <p
          class="main-collection__count"
          data-results-count
          data-results-total="{{ paginate.items }}"
          aria-live="polite"
        >
          {%- comment -%}
            `paginate.items`, not `collection.products_count` — the latter is the
            collection's own size and ignores active filters, so a filtered page
            showing 12 cards would announce all 84.

            Dawn's pluralised key gives "12 products" where the board reads
            "12 Products". The wording loses to correctness: this string ships
            translated into every locale the theme carries, and a hardcoded
            "Products" would be English on a Spanish storefront and would say
            "1 Products" besides.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {{- 'products.facets.product_count_simple' | t: count: paginate.items -}}
        </p>

        {%- if collection.filters.size > 0 or collection.sort_options.size > 0 -%}
          <div class="main-collection__tools">
            {%- comment -%}
              Filter sits left of Sort, per the board. `aria-controls` is what
              binds it to the panel — bf-filter-drawer.js collects its toggles
              by querying for that attribute, so no id needs to be threaded
              through in the other direction.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if collection.filters.size > 0 -%}
              <button
                class="main-collection__filter"
                type="button"
                aria-controls="bf-filter-drawer"
                aria-expanded="false"
              >
                <span class="main-collection__filter-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                  {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'sliders-horizontal', size: 18 -%}
                </span>
                {{ section.settings.filter_label }}
              </button>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if collection.sort_options.size > 0 -%}
              <div class="main-collection__sort">
                {%- assign current_sort = collection.sort_by | default: collection.default_sort_by -%}
                <label class="visually-hidden" for="bf-collection-sort">
                  {{ 'products.facets.sort_by_label' | t }}
                </label>
                <select
                  class="main-collection__sort-select"
                  id="bf-collection-sort"
                  name="sort_by"
                  data-results-sort
                >
                  {%- for option in collection.sort_options -%}
                    <option
                      value="{{ option.value }}"
                      {% if option.value == current_sort %}
                        selected
                      {% endif %}
                    >
                      {{ option.name }}
                    </option>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </select>

                {%- comment -%}
                  Mirrors the select's current option as text, because the board
                  draws a label and a chevron and a native select cannot be
                  painted that way across browsers. The server renders the correct
                  value so a no-JS first paint is right; the script updates it.

                  "Sort by:" rather than the board's "Sort:" — it is the key the
                  search page's toolbar already uses, and two sibling toolbars
                  disagreeing by one word is worse than one word differing from
                  the board. It is also translated, which a literal would not be.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <span class="main-collection__sort-label" data-results-sort-label>
                  {{ 'products.facets.sort_by_label' | t }}
                  {%- for option in collection.sort_options -%}
                    {%- if option.value == current_sort %} {{ option.name }}{% endif -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </span>
                <span class="main-collection__sort-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                  {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'chevron-down', size: 16 -%}
                </span>
              </div>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Everything a filter or sort change replaces, in one element. The script
        fetches this section for the new URL and swaps this subtree wholesale
        rather than diffing card by card — the server already decided what the
        page contains, and re-deciding it here is how a grid and its count drift
        apart.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="main-collection__results" data-results>
        {%- if collection.products.size > 0 -%}
          <div class="main-collection__grid" data-results-grid>
            {%- for product in collection.products -%}
              {%- render 'bf-product-card',
                product: product,
                ratio: '3 / 4',
                sizes: '(min-width: 834px) 25vw, 50vw',
                index: forloop.index0
              -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            The next page's URL, rendered by the server. The script reads it,
            fetches that page of this section, appends its cards and replaces
            this value with the one the fetched page carried — so paging forward
            needs no arithmetic on the client. Shopify keeps the active filters
            and sort in `paginate.next.url`, so a filtered Load More stays
            filtered without this having to rebuild the query string.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div
            class="main-collection__more"
            data-results-more
            {% unless paginate.next %}
              hidden
            {% endunless %}
          >
            <button
              class="main-collection__more-button"
              type="button"
              data-results-more-button
              data-next-url="{{ paginate.next.url }}"
            >
              <span>{{ section.settings.load_more_label }}</span>
              <span class="main-collection__more-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'plus', size: 18 -%}
              </span>
            </button>
          </div>

        {%- else -%}
          <div class="main-collection__empty">
            <p class="main-collection__empty-lines">
              <span class="main-collection__empty-line">{{ section.settings.empty_line_1 }}</span>
              <span class="main-collection__empty-line">{{ section.settings.empty_line_2 }}</span>
            </p>

            <a
              class="main-collection__cta"
              href="{{ section.settings.empty_cta_url | default: routes.all_products_collection_url }}"
            >
              {{ section.settings.empty_cta_label }}
              <span class="main-collection__cta-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'arrow-up-right', size: 48 -%}
              </span>
            </a>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Inside <bf-main-collection> so the drawer can reach the grid with a
      closest() rather than a document-wide id lookup, but OUTSIDE
      [data-results] so a filter change does not swap away the panel the visitor
      is still using. It is `position: fixed`, so where it sits in the document
      costs nothing in layout.

      `paginate.items` is passed explicitly: snippets get their own scope and
      cannot see `paginate`, which is a block-local object rather than a global.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if collection.filters.size > 0 -%}
      {%- render 'bf-filter-drawer',
        filters: collection.filters,
        total: paginate.items,
        heading: section.settings.filter_heading,
        clear_label: section.settings.filter_clear_label,
        apply_label: section.settings.filter_apply_label,
        close_label: section.settings.filter_close_label,
        chip_filters: section.settings.chip_filters
      -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </bf-main-collection>
{%- endpaginate -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Collection",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "breadcrumb_home",
      "label": "Breadcrumb home link",
      "default": "Home"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "breadcrumb_label",
      "label": "Breadcrumb landmark name",
      "info": "Read by screen readers to announce the navigation. Not shown.",
      "default": "Breadcrumb"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "products_per_page",
      "label": "Products per page",
      "info": "The board shows three rows of four.",
      "min": 4,
      "max": 36,
      "step": 4,
      "default": 12
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "load_more_label",
      "label": "Load more button",
      "default": "Load More"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Filters"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "filter_label",
      "label": "Toolbar trigger",
      "default": "Filter"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "filter_heading",
      "label": "Drawer heading",
      "default": "Filters"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "filter_clear_label",
      "label": "Reset button",
      "default": "Clear All"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "filter_apply_label",
      "label": "Confirm button",
      "info": "The live result count is appended in brackets.",
      "default": "Show Results"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "filter_close_label",
      "label": "Close button name",
      "info": "Read by screen readers. Not shown.",
      "default": "Close filters"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "chip_filters",
      "label": "Filters drawn as chips",
      "info": "Comma-separated filter names. These render as a grid of boxes instead of checkbox rows — for short values like sizes. Filters are added in Shopify admin under Search & Discovery.",
      "default": "Size"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Empty collection"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "empty_line_1",
      "label": "First line",
      "default": "Nothing in this collection yet."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "empty_line_2",
      "label": "Second line",
      "default": "Try another one."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "empty_cta_label",
      "label": "Link label",
      "default": "Explore the shop"
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "empty_cta_url",
      "label": "Link",
      "info": "Defaults to all products."
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
